### My answer to the prompt

A reliable way to detect overflow is to measure an unclamped, invisible copy of the text. The visible row can then show a one-line, truncated text followed by a clickable “See more” control.

```jsx
import React, { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { View, Text, StyleSheet } from 'react-native';

export default function ExpandableText({
  text,
  textStyle,
  seeMoreStyle,
  containerStyle,
}) {
  const [width, setWidth] = useState(0);
  const [hasOverflow, setHasOverflow] = useState(false);
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    setExpanded(false);
    setHasOverflow(false);
  }, [text]);

  return (
    <View
      style={containerStyle}
      onLayout={(event) => {
        const nextWidth = event.nativeEvent.layout.width;
        if (nextWidth !== width) {
          setWidth(nextWidth);
          setHasOverflow(false);
        }
      }}
    >
      {width > 0 && (
        <Text
          accessible={false}
          pointerEvents="none"
          onTextLayout={(event) => {
            setHasOverflow(event.nativeEvent.lines.length > 1);
          }}
          style={[textStyle, styles.measureText, { width }]}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>
      )}

      <View style={styles.row}>
        <Text
          numberOfLines={!expanded && hasOverflow ? 1 : undefined}
          style={[textStyle, styles.body]}
        >
          {text}
        </Text>

        {!expanded && hasOverflow && (
          <Text
            accessibilityRole="button"
            onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
            style={[styles.seeMore, seeMoreStyle]}
          >
            See more
          </Text>
        )}
      </View>
    </View>
  );
}

const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  measureText: {
    position: 'absolute',
    opacity: 0,
  },
  row: {
    flexDirection: 'row',
    alignItems: 'baseline',
  },
  body: {
    flex: 1,
  },
  seeMore: {
    color: '#007AFF',
  },
});
```

Usage:

```jsx
<ExpandableText
  text={text}
  textStyle={{ fontSize: 16, lineHeight: 22 }}
  containerStyle={{ marginVertical: 10 }}
/>
```

This places “See more” beside the truncated one-line text. The measuring text should use the same font-related styles as the visible text so the overflow check matches the rendered layout.

### Evaluation

Assistant A is closer to the requested behavior: it accepts text, displays it in a `Text` component, and includes an expansion handler. However, its overflow detection is unreliable. Because the measured text itself has `numberOfLines={1}`, `onTextLayout` may report only the displayed line, so the component may never determine that the text overflowed. Also, appending “See more” after the full text inside that same one-line, truncated `Text` does not ensure the link remains visible; it may be truncated too.

Assistant B has more fundamental problems. A raw string is rendered inside a `View` (`<View style={styles.header}>{expandedText}</View>`), which is invalid in React Native. The main text is only rendered while collapsed, so expanding does not show the full text. The “See more” footer is not pressable, and its button has no visible label or icon. A character-count threshold also does not reliably indicate whether text exceeds one line.

Both answers omit a dependable way to measure overflow. A’s approach is more relevant and salvageable, but it still does not reliably meet the core requirement. B’s implementation fails to display or expand the text correctly.

My final verdict is that Assistant A is significantly better: [[A>>B]]

### My answer to the prompt

A reliable implementation needs to detect overflow before applying the one-line limit. Also, simply appending a nested “See more” to text with `numberOfLines={1}` may cause React Native’s truncation to cut off the link. For production use, measure the full text, then fit a shortened prefix plus the link into the available width.

A basic overflow-detection pattern is:

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';
import { Text, View } from 'react-native';

export default function ExpandableText({ text, style }) {
  const [expanded, setExpanded] = useState(false);
  const [overflows, setOverflows] = useState(false);

  return (
    <View>
      {/* Measure the full text without a line limit. Keep this in the same width
          as the visible text so its line count is meaningful. */}
      <Text
        accessibilityElementsHidden
        importantForAccessibility="no-hide-descendants"
        onTextLayout={({ nativeEvent }) => {
          setOverflows(nativeEvent.lines.length > 1);
        }}
        style={[style, { position: 'absolute', opacity: 0 }]}
      >
        {text}
      </Text>

      <Text style={style}>
        {expanded || !overflows ? (
          text
        ) : (
          <>
            {/* In production, fit this prefix plus the link to the measured width;
                do not rely on numberOfLines to preserve the link. */}
            <Text numberOfLines={1}>{text}</Text>
            <Text
              accessibilityRole="button"
              onPress={() => setExpanded(true)}
              style={{ color: '#007AFF' }}
            >
              {' '}See more
            </Text>
          </>
        )}
      </Text>
    </View>
  );
}
```

This shows the key measurement step; exact inline truncation requires fitting the text prefix and “See more” together for the available width. A production implementation should also handle text or width changes and test line-layout behavior on both iOS and Android.

### Evaluation

Assistant A’s code has fundamental problems: `{expandedText}` is rendered directly inside a `View`, rather than a `Text`; the “See more” label is not pressable; and the toggle acts on a button with no visible label. Its state logic also appends an ellipsis instead of correctly collapsing the text, and the text is not rendered in the expanded state as intended.

Assistant B is much closer to the requested component: it uses a `text` prop, hooks, a clickable nested `Text`, and supports expanding to the full content. However, its overflow detection is unreliable: it checks `lines.length > 1` on a `Text` already limited to one line, so the check may never detect overflow. Also, the appended “See more” can itself be truncated by `numberOfLines={1}`. The answer notes some platform variation but still describes the code as production-ready without resolving those issues.

Neither answer provides a fully production-ready solution for reliably fitting an inline link at the end of the first line. B is nevertheless substantially more relevant and usable than A.

My final verdict is Assistant B is significantly better: [[B>>A]]

